This version uses DFS instead of a queue. Each cell stores the minute when it becomes rotten. If another path reaches the same orange later, that path is ignored. If it reaches the orange sooner, DFS updates the time and explores again.

The initial rotten value is treated as time `2`, so a fresh orange reached directly gets value `3`. At the end, subtracting `2` gives the real number of minutes. An orange still equal to `1` means it was unreachable.

A useful edge case is a grid with fresh oranges but no rotten oranges. No DFS call can start, so the final scan returns `-1`. A grid with no fresh oranges returns `0`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.length == 0 || grid[0].length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = grid.length;
        int cols = grid[0].length;

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                if (grid[row][col] == 2) {
                    spread(grid, row, col, 2);
                }
            }
        }

        int latest = 2;
        for (int[] row : grid) {
            for (int cell : row) {
                if (cell == 1) {
                    return -1;
                }
                latest = Math.max(latest, cell);
            }
        }

        return latest - 2;
    }

    private void spread(int[][] grid, int row, int col, int minute) {
        if (row < 0 || row >= grid.length ||
            col < 0 || col >= grid[0].length ||
            grid[row][col] == 0) {
            return;
        }

        if (grid[row][col] > 1 && grid[row][col] < minute) {
            return;
        }

        grid[row][col] = minute;

        spread(grid, row + 1, col, minute + 1);
        spread(grid, row - 1, col, minute + 1);
        spread(grid, row, col + 1, minute + 1);
        spread(grid, row, col - 1, minute + 1);
    }
}
```

For a chain such as `[[2,1,1]]`, the values become `2,3,4`, so the answer is `4 - 2 = 2`.

Time complexity: `O((rows * cols)^2)` in the worst case because a better route can cause another DFS spread. The common cases are faster.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the recursion stack. The grid is changed in place.

This is a simple simulation without a BFS queue. At the start of every minute, I collect the positions of all rotten oranges. I then use only that list to spread the rot. This keeps an orange that just became rotten from spreading again during the same minute.

After each pass, the fresh count is reduced for every new orange. If a full minute makes no progress, the remaining oranges are separated from the rotten ones and the answer is `-1`.

The method is easy to follow, but it scans the whole grid again for every minute, so it is slower than the queue version.

```cpp
class Solution{
public:
    int orangesRotting(vector<vector<int>> &grid) {
        if (grid.empty() || grid[0].empty()) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = grid.size();
        int cols = grid[0].size();
        int fresh = 0;

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; ++row) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; ++col) {
                if (grid[row][col] == 1) {
                    ++fresh;
                }
            }
        }

        if (fresh == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int minutes = 0;
        int directions[4][2] = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

        while (fresh > 0) {
            vector<pair<int, int>> currentRotten;

            for (int row = 0; row < rows; ++row) {
                for (int col = 0; col < cols; ++col) {
                    if (grid[row][col] == 2) {
                        currentRotten.push_back({row, col});
                    }
                }
            }

            if (currentRotten.empty()) {
                return -1;
            }

            int becameRotten = 0;

            for (auto orange : currentRotten) {
                for (auto &direction : directions) {
                    int nextRow = orange.first + direction[0];
                    int nextCol = orange.second + direction[1];

                    if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                        nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    if (grid[nextRow][nextCol] != 1) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    grid[nextRow][nextCol] = 2;
                    --fresh;
                    ++becameRotten;
                }
            }

            if (becameRotten == 0) {
                return -1;
            }

            ++minutes;
        }

        return minutes;
    }
};
```

