Instead of storing the whole grid in the search structure, this version keeps two sets: fresh coordinates and the current rotten frontier. Each minute builds the next frontier from the current one.

Removing a coordinate from `fresh` marks it as handled. This also means two rotten neighbors cannot count the same orange twice. The original grid is not changed in this version.

One edge case is an empty grid, which returns `0`. If the frontier becomes empty while the fresh set still has cells, there is no path left and the result is `-1`.

```java
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

class Solution {
    public int orangesRotting(int[][] grid) {
        if (grid == null || grid.length == 0 || grid[0].length == 0) {
            return 0;
        }

        int rows = grid.length;
        int cols = grid[0].length;
        Set<Integer> fresh = new HashSet<>();
        Set<Integer> frontier = new HashSet<>();

        for (int row = 0; row < rows; row++) {
            for (int col = 0; col < cols; col++) {
                int id = row * cols + col;
                if (grid[row][col] == 1) {
                    fresh.add(id);
                } else if (grid[row][col] == 2) {
                    frontier.add(id);
                }
            }
        }

        int minutes = 0;
        int[][] directions = {{1, 0}, {-1, 0}, {0, 1}, {0, -1}};

        while (!fresh.isEmpty() && !frontier.isEmpty()) {
            Set<Integer> nextFrontier = new HashSet<>();

            for (int id : frontier) {
                int row = id / cols;
                int col = id % cols;

                for (int[] direction : directions) {
                    int nextRow = row + direction[0];
                    int nextCol = col + direction[1];

                    if (nextRow < 0 || nextRow >= rows ||
                        nextCol < 0 || nextCol >= cols) {
                        continue;
                    }

                    int nextId = nextRow * cols + nextCol;
                    if (fresh.remove(nextId)) {
                        nextFrontier.add(nextId);
                    }
                }
            }

            frontier = nextFrontier;
            minutes++;
        }

        return fresh.isEmpty() ? minutes : -1;
    }
}
```

For a dry run on `[[2,1,0],[0,1,1]]`, the first frontier reaches the top-right fresh cell, and the next frontier continues through the bottom cells. The set of fresh coordinates becomes empty after three minutes.

Time complexity: `O(rows * cols)` on average with hash sets.

Space complexity: `O(rows * cols)` for the fresh and frontier sets. The input grid is left unchanged.

